  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 135 PM CDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nThis afternoon and into the evening, widely scattered rain showers\nare expected to move across far northern Minnesota. These will be\nhigh-based showers, likely only producing sprinkles or a few\nhundredths of an inch of new rainfall. In addition to the light\nprecipitation, wind gusts of 25 to 35 mph will persist into this\nevening across the area. By late this evening, the winds will \ndiminish as the showers fade.\n\nThursday will bring less wind, dry weather, and mild conditions to\nthe Northland. High temperatures will still remain slightly above\nnormal for this time of year. A warming trend will then begin on\nFriday, as dry conditions persist across the region and flow\nshifts southerly ahead of an approaching system.\n\nFor this time of year, an extremely warm period sets up for\nSaturday. High temperatures are forecast to be 15 to 25 degrees\nabove normal, reaching into the upper 70s to lower 80s across the\nregion. A few spots in northwest Wisconsin cannot be ruled out to\nhit the mid 80s. These forecast temperatures place a few climate\ndata spots within a few degrees of reaching daily maximum high\ntemperatures for October 10. Saturday southerly winds will gust 25\nto 35 mph areawide. A Wind Advisory may be needed for the South\nShore where gusts to 45 mph are possible (50-70 percent chance) due\nto the tightest pressure gradient in-place there and areas\neastward. Winds will be lighter further west into north-central\nMinnesota.\n\nA lingering very warm sector will remain over east-central\nMinnesota, the Arrowhead, and across the entirety of northwest\nWisconsin on Sunday. Meanwhile, a cooler airmass will push into\nnorth-central Minnesota Sunday, setting up a tight temperature\ngradient and a decrease in high temperatures. Meaningful rain\nshowers are forecast to begin later Sunday night, primarily for\nfar northern Minnesota, ahead of a more significant rainfall\nchance Monday.\n\nRain showers will become much more widespread on Monday afternoon.\nA gusty and seasonably cool airmass will work into the area on\nnorthwest wrap-around flow as high pressure sets up over the\nsouthern Canadian prairies. By Tuesday, cold air advection rain\nshowers remain possible during the daytime as the much cooler\nairmass lingers across the region.",
